title: pirate_port_context
---
// The Pirate Port's conversations (gate address SAILING), one node per
// resident, named `pirate_port_<resident>`. The rooms are written by
// `tools/ambition_ldtk_tools/specs/pirate_port/make_specs.py`.
//
// The problem of the port: the wind took a page of Ines's freight log to the
// crow's nest of the brig (flag `pirate_port_manifest_found`, the page's
// pickup). Handing it back sets `pirate_port_manifest_returned`; the quest
// `pirate_port_lost_manifest` pays the reward.
//
// Never played. No placement names this node; it declares the zone.
===

title: pirate_port_ines
---
<<if condition("world.flag_set", "pirate_port_manifest_returned")>>
Ines: Page eleven, back in the book. The goat is logged, the limes are logged, and you are logged as "helpful".
-> What came through today?
    Ines: Two crates of lamp oil, a mapmaker with a nosebleed, and a letter for "whoever is in charge". I filed it under me.
-> Has anyone asked for the passenger list?
    Ines: A broker sent a letter asking for the names of everyone arriving from Castle Godwin's side. Didn't say who he worked for.
    Ines: I asked him to come in person. Haven't seen him.
-> Carry on.
<<elseif condition("world.flag_set", "pirate_port_manifest_found")>>
Ines: Is that page eleven? From the crow's nest? You climbed the Persistence for a page of my log?
-> Hand her the page.
    <<command "world.set_flag" "pirate_port_manifest_returned" true>>
    Ines: Every crate in the warehouse has an owner again. Take this from the clerk's tin. Do not tell the harbour master it was a tin.
-> Keep it a moment longer.
    Ines: It is not a souvenir. It is a legal document with a gull print on it.
<<else>>
Ines: Ring freight this way. Declare it, sign it, and keep it away from the edge.
-> You look worried.
    Ines: The wind took page eleven of the freight log. It went up past the brig's yards and caught in her crow's nest. Without it, half the warehouse belongs to nobody.
    Ines: The brig is at the end of the pier. Mind the gaps in the boards. The harbour keeps what it gets.
-> What do you log?
    Ines: Everything the ring brings. Crates, letters, a goat last week. The ring does not care what it carries. I do.
-> What happens when the gate gets busy?
    Ines: Passenger traffic goes first. I get the warehouse after the last sick traveller has found a bench.
    Ines: There were three fishing crews waiting for rope on Tuesday. One went out without it.
-> Carry on.
<<endif>>
===

title: pirate_port_tomas
---
Tomas: Do not mind me. I am waiting for my stomach. It is still in the other dimension.
-> Is the gate always like this?
    Tomas: For people, yes. Three seconds of nothing, then the floor arrives all at once. You are a machine. You probably felt a draught.
-> Where are you going?
    Tomas: To sell lamp oil to pirates. The ring made it a short trip. Nobody said it would make it a long recovery.
-> Does everyone come through the ring now?
    Tomas: My brother still sails. He can eat lunch underway, for one thing.
    Tomas: I've got invoices to deliver on the station tomorrow. I may sleep here tonight.
-> Feel better.
===

title: pirate_port_agnes
---
Agnes: Rope, tar, biscuits, remedies. Forty years on this street. The ring changed the customers, not the rope.
-> Ginger for the gate-sick?
    Agnes: Your friend in the plaza has bought three bags. He is not better. He is fond of ginger now.
-> Buy Health Cell — 8g <<if can_afford(8)>>
    <<buy_item "healthcell" 8>>
    Agnes: Good for what ails a body. Even yours.
-> Buy Spare Battery — 12g <<if can_afford(12)>>
    <<buy_item "sparebattery" 12>>
    Agnes: Came through the ring last week. Fresh from somewhere.
-> Who buys all this rope now?
    Agnes: Fishermen. Dock crews. Station mechanics came down for a coil last week. Took two hours explaining which way their pulley turns.
-> Do you have anything for a sick traveller?
    Agnes: Ginger, water, a chair by the door. That'll be three coppers. The chair's free.
-> Just looking.
===

title: pirate_port_jory
---
Jory: Mind the boards. There are two gaps in the pier and the harbour is deeper than it looks.
-> What do you catch?
    Jory: Whatever this sea gives. Now the market sells fish from three seas, through the ring. Mine still taste of this one.
-> What is up the brig's mast?
    Jory: The crow's nest of the Persistence. Gulls, rope and, today, a piece of paper. Ines will want that.
-> Has the ring changed fishing?
    Jory: I can sell a catch on three worlds before supper. Used to be I knew every buyer by his hat.
    Jory: Agnes says I should charge more. I know what my neighbours can pay.
-> Carry on.
===

title: pirate_port_bram
---
Bram: Harbour master. The docks are mine, the cove is the crews', and the sky over it is anyone's who can fly.
-> What is past that door?
    Bram: The pirate cove. Mind your manners there. The crews keep a lookout above it, where the skyship raiders gather. They do not like visitors.
-> Does the ring bother you?
    Bram: A ship takes a week to bring what the ring brings in a breath. I still trust the ship. The ship never felt sick.
-> Is the cove safe?
    Bram: Depends who you ask. The Admiral pays his mooring fees. His raiders are less reliable.
    Bram: I send Jory in to collect. Nobody wants to start a fight with an old fisherman.
-> Do cargo ships still earn their keep?
    Bram: Small ones do. Long hauls are harder. Freight buyers want the gate price, then they want the ship price when the ring goes down.
-> Carry on.
===

title: pirate_port_wren
---
Wren: Welcome to The Drowned Compass. We never tell you where you are, which is why you come back.
-> What do you serve?
    Wren: Grog, chowder, and a tea for the gate-sick that is mostly ginger and hope.
-> Who is the big one?
    Wren: Bess, from the cove. She pays, she sings, and nobody asks about the cannon port in her blade.
-> What's everyone arguing about?
    Wren: Bram raised the dock fees. Jory says he isn't paying. Bram says Jory never pays.
    Wren: They'll be in here after sunset to sort it out over supper.
-> Has Bess always come here?
    Wren: Since she was small enough to sit on the counter. Last night she bought the whole room supper. She wouldn't say why.
-> Carry on.
===

title: pirate_port_bess
---
Broadside Bess: Shore leave. A table, a chowder, and nobody to board. Do not ruin it.
-> Do you crew from the cove?
    Broadside Bess: The cove is home. The port is where the money is. The sky is where the trouble is.
-> Have you been through the ring?
    Broadside Bess: Once. I came out the other side and fired a broadside at the floor. On purpose. Probably.
-> Why work with ninjas?
    Broadside Bess: Bird stole the ninja chief's fancy footwork. Stole our songs too. Admiral and Shadow Oni made a deal.
    Broadside Bess: Been told to keep my mouth shut while we share a roof. I've managed half a day.
-> What did the bird take from you?
    Broadside Bess: Old recordings. Annet's mother singing, before she lost her voice. They're in that chest. Bring it back if you find it.
-> Enjoy the chowder.
===

title: pirate_port_bolt
---
Tamsin Bolt: Mind the sail, love, it's someone's whole livelihood and it's on the floor. What can I sew you?
-> What are the flags on the wall?
    Tamsin Bolt: Orders. Every captain wants a skull and every captain wants it to be theirs. Eyepatch for Captain Vane. Monocle for the navigator. Moustache for, well, the quartermaster has asked me not to say.
    Tamsin Bolt: The party hat was for a birthday. It flew for a week. Nobody fired on that ship once.
-> Why was the smiling one sent back?
    Tamsin Bolt: Too friendly. Ships kept sailing up to say hello. Salt Annet said it was bad for business. I thought it was the best thing I ever made.
-> Do you sew sails too?
    Tamsin Bolt: Mostly sails. Flags are the fun. A sail's three weeks of seams and nobody looks at it. A flag's a day, and everyone looks at it, and then they run.
-> I'll come back for a flag.
    Tamsin Bolt: Bring me a face. I'll put it on a skull. Everyone looks better on a skull.
===

title: pirate_port_lamplugh
---
Ezra Lamplugh: Evening. Or morning. Up here it's whichever the lamp says. Mind the wreckers on the rocks; they don't like visitors who leave.
-> Why do the wreckers want your light out?
    Ezra Lamplugh: Their lantern on the shore says HARBOUR to a ship in the dark. Mine says ROCKS. A captain can only believe one of us.
    Ezra Lamplugh: The Good Ship Probably believed theirs. I've written to the captain's widow. Twice. She keeps sending me pies.
-> How do you keep it lit?
    Ezra Lamplugh: Oil, wick and a lens the size of a cart wheel, polished every morning. One hundred and twelve steps up. I count them so I don't have to think.
-> Do you get lonely out here?
    Ezra Lamplugh: I have the cabbages, the gulls, and every ship that doesn't sink. That's a lot of company, if you look at it right.
-> Keep it burning.
    Ezra Lamplugh: Always. That's the whole job. Keep it burning and tell the truth.
===

title: pirate_port_brine
---
Nell Brine: Mind the glass. That is not litter, that is stock.
-> What do you sell?
    Nell Brine: Whatever the sea gives up. A ship's bell, a captain's hat, a painting of a captain wearing the hat.
    Nell Brine: All of it authentic salvage. I certify it myself, which saves everyone the trouble.
-> Who wrecked that ship?
    Nell Brine: The lads with the false lighthouse. They hang a lantern and a mirror and call it a harbour.
    Nell Brine: The Good Ship Probably believed them. Nobody else has, since. They are not good at business.
-> Why stay out here?
    Nell Brine: The port charges rent. The shore charges nothing, and every tide is a delivery.
-> Good luck with the tides.
    Nell Brine: Luck is for gamblers. I have a calendar of them.
===
